WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:19.130
I had exposure to mathematics at a very early
age.

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:25.660
My father is a mathematician, so I was always
interested in what my father was doing.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:29.660
In school I was naturally drawn by mathematics
and physics.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:36.060
My PhD was in the physics department, but it was really a PhD in mathematics.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:42.440
I was always attracted to the fact that the
statements in mathematics are permanent.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:53.740
The field I'm in is essentially at the interface
between probability theory and mathematical

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:57.640
analysis or theory of partial differential
equations.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:06.370
And one of the big questions in the field
is this question of universality which says

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:14.621
that many systems exhibit very similar, essentially
identical, large scale behavior although the

00:01:14.621 --> 00:01:17.590
microscopic descriptions are very, very different.

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:23.470
There are some situations where this large-scale
behavior is not described by a Gaussian object,

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:27.110
and these are extremely poorly understood.

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:32.799
I was always interested in the physics of
sound — understanding what sounds are, what

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:34.899
they look like or what they're made of.

00:01:34.899 --> 00:01:41.950
So there is a very indirect connection between
the analysis of sound and the two books that

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:48.679
I've been developing for analyzing these posed
equations, which is that to some extent both

00:01:48.679 --> 00:01:50.380
of them rely on wavelet analysis.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:56.850
So I was walking to my office when I suddenly
realized that wavelet analysis would give

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:04.329
me a tool that allows me to go from one-dimensional
systems to higher-dimensional systems, and

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:10.049
then also make the analysis that I'd previously
done on one-dimensional systems more robust.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.700
You can really get an idea anywhere, at any
moment.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.940
It doesn't have to be in the office.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:26.780
It really helps to kind of do something completely
different — listen to music, do some cooking

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.350
— but still keep it at the back of your
mind.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:37.490
I think it's really important to do genuinely
curiosity-driven type of mathematics.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:42.940
I think it's only by being genuinely curious
about the problem and genuinely interested

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:49.350
in the problem that you will be able to keep
it in your head for sufficiently long time

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:54.459
or sort of think about it in a sufficiently
focused way to actually make some real progress.

